  "schema_version": 1,
  "kind": "lesson",
  "items": [
    {
      "external_id": "C11-CHEM-CH7-7.4.3",
      "chapter": "pk-punjab/xi/chemistry/c11-chem/ch7",
      "topic": "pk-punjab/xi/chemistry/c11-chem/ch7/7.4.3",
      "title": "7.4.3 Types of reaction order",
      "language": "en",
      "source_refs": [
        {
          "source_document_id": "b07b1f6c-f5a4-509d-ae86-73be5bffb0d4",
          "pdf_from": 155,
          "pdf_to": 156,
          "note": "Topic 7.4.3, printed pages 155-156, as printed: zero, first, second, third and fractional order, each under its own heading, with the rate equations and examples as equation blocks."
        }
      ],
      "body": {
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": 3,
            "text": "Zero Order Reaction"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "The rate of a zero order reaction is independent of the concentration of the reactants. A change in the concentration has no effect on the speed of the reaction."
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "For the general reaction:"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\text{A} \\longrightarrow \\text{Product}",
            "text_alt": "A → Product"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\text{Rate} = k[\\text{A}]^{0}",
            "text_alt": "Rate = k[A]⁰"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Examples:"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\text{i)}\\quad \\ce{H2_{(g)} + Cl2 -> 2HCl_{(g)}}",
            "text_alt": "i) H₂(g) + Cl₂ → 2HCl(g)"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\text{Rate} = k[\\ce{H2}]^{0}[\\ce{Cl2}]^{0} = k",
            "text_alt": "Rate = k[H₂]⁰[Cl₂]⁰ = k"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\text{ii)}\\quad \\ce{2NH3 -> N2 + 3H2}",
            "text_alt": "ii) 2NH₃ → N₂ + 3H₂"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\text{Rate} = k[\\ce{NH3}]^{0} = k",
            "text_alt": "Rate = k[NH₃]⁰ = k"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Photochemical reactions are usually zero order."
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": 3,
            "text": "First Order Reaction"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "In these reactions, there may be multiple reactants present, but concentration of only one reactant will change during the reaction. Examples of a first order reaction is:"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Decomposition of nitrogen pentoxide involves the following equation."
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\ce{2N2O5_{(g)} -> 2N2O4_{(g)} + O2_{(g)}}",
            "text_alt": "2N₂O₅(g) → 2N₂O₄(g) + O₂(g)"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "The experimentally determined rate equation for this reaction is as follows:"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\text{Rate} = k[\\ce{N2O5}] \\qquad (n = 1)",
            "text_alt": "Rate = k[N₂O₅]   (n = 1)"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "This equation suggests that the reaction is first order with respect to the concentration of N₂O₅."
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": 3,
            "text": "Second Order Reaction"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "A reaction for which sum of the exponents of the concentrations in the rate equation is 2. A second order reaction is a reaction whose rate depends either on the concentration of one reactant raised to the second power or on the concentrations of two different reactants, each raised to the first power. The simpler type of reaction involves one kind of reactant molecule."
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\text{Rate} = k[\\text{A}]^{2} \\qquad (n = 2)",
            "text_alt": "Rate = k[A]²   (n = 2)"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\text{Rate} = k[\\text{A}]^{1}[\\text{B}]^{1} \\qquad (n = 1+1 = 2)",
            "text_alt": "Rate = k[A]¹[B]¹   (n = 1+1 = 2)"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Oxidation of nitric oxide with ozone has been shown to be first order with respect to NO and first order with respect to O₃. The sum of the individual orders gives the overall order of reaction as two."
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\ce{NO_{(g)} + O3_{(g)} -> NO2_{(g)} + O2_{(g)}} \\qquad \\text{Rate} = k[\\ce{NO}][\\ce{O3}]",
            "text_alt": "NO(g) + O₃(g) → NO₂(g) + O₂(g)   Rate = k[NO][O₃]"
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": 3,
            "text": "Third Order Reaction"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "A third order reaction is the reaction for which sum of the exponents of the concentrations in the rate equation is three."
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\text{Rate} = k[\\text{A}]^{3} \\qquad (n = 3)",
            "text_alt": "Rate = k[A]³   (n = 3)"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\text{or Rate} = k[\\text{A}]^{2}[\\text{B}]^{1} \\qquad (n = 2+1 = 3)",
            "text_alt": "or Rate = k[A]²[B]¹   (n = 2+1 = 3)"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\text{or Rate} = k[\\text{A}]^{1}[\\text{B}]^{1}[\\text{C}]^{1} \\qquad (n = 1+1+1 = 3)",
            "text_alt": "or Rate = k[A]¹[B]¹[C]¹   (n = 1+1+1 = 3)"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\ce{2FeCl3_{(aq)} + 6KI_{(aq)} -> 2FeI2_{(aq)} + 6KCl_{(aq)} + I2}",
            "text_alt": "2FeCl₃(aq) + 6KI(aq) → 2FeI₂(aq) + 6KCl(aq) + I₂"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "This reaction involves eight reactant molecules but experimentally it has been found to be a third order reaction."
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\text{Rate} = k[\\ce{FeCl3}][\\ce{KI}]^{2}",
            "text_alt": "Rate = k[FeCl₃][KI]²"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "also, the following reaction is third order"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\ce{2NO_{(g)} + O2_{(g)} -> 2NO2_{(g)}} \\qquad \\text{Rate} = k[\\ce{NO}]^{2}[\\ce{O2}]^{1}",
            "text_alt": "2NO(g) + O₂(g) → 2NO₂(g)   Rate = k[NO]²[O₂]¹"
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": 3,
            "text": "Fractional Order Reaction"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "A reaction in which the sum of exponents of rate equation is in fraction, is called the fractional order reaction. For example, consider the formation of carbon tetrachloride from chloroform."
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\ce{CHCl3_{(l)} + Cl2_{(g)} -> CCl4_{(l)} + HCl_{(g)}}",
            "text_alt": "CHCl₃(l) + Cl₂(g) → CCl₄(l) + HCl(g)"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\text{Rate} = k[\\ce{CHCl3}][\\ce{Cl2}]^{1/2} \\qquad n = 1 + 1/2 = 1.5",
            "text_alt": "Rate = k[CHCl₃][Cl₂]¹ᐟ²   n = 1 + 1/2 = 1.5"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Reactions involving free radicals frequently exhibit fractional orders."
          }
        ]
      }
    }
  ]
}
